comments.body:
Ran into this one myself. Fixed it locally — same function you identified, the `#[cfg(windows)]` `pread_exact` in `opened_file.rs`.

`seek_read` doesn't error on short reads; at EOF it just returns `Ok(0)`. The old code ignored the count, so an empty or missing file passed the initial hash check as if it contained valid data (and every new torrent on Windows paid ~20s for the fake verification before actually downloading).

The fix loops until the buffer is filled and fails with `UnexpectedEof` on short reads:

```rust
#[cfg(windows)]
fn pread_exact(&self, offset: u64, buf: &mut [u8]) -> anyhow::Result<()> {
    use std::os::windows::fs::FileExt;

    let mut remaining = buf.len();
    let mut buf = buf;
    let mut offset = offset;
    while remaining > 0 {
        let read = self.seek_read(&mut buf[..remaining], offset)?;
        if read == 0 {
            return Err(std::io::Error::new(
                std::io::ErrorKind::UnexpectedEof,
                "failed to fill whole buffer (file missing or truncated)",
            )
            .into());
        }
        remaining -= read;
        offset += read as u64;
        buf = &mut buf[read..];
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

Same shape as the Windows `pwrite_all` already in that file, and semantically aligned with the Unix `read_exact_at` branch. I also added a few Windows-gated tests (`#[cfg(all(test, windows))]`, unit-level plus one through `FilesystemStorage`) covering this — they fail on the old code and pass with the fix.

Happy to turn it into a PR if that's useful.

body:
### Problem

When embedding `librqbit` as a library, there's no public way to learn where a torrent's data actually lives on disk. `ManagedTorrent::shared().options` is `pub(crate)`, and the resolved `output_folder` lives inside `ManagedTorrentOptions`, so external code can't read it. (Still the case in `9.0.0-rc.0`.)

The session-wide default is available (`Session::new(default_output_folder)`), but the *per-torrent* resolved folder isn't — and they differ whenever a torrent is added with an `output_folder`/`sub_folder` override, or gets the auto-subfolder for a multi-file torrent.

### Workaround (and why it's fragile)

I reconstruct the path from the session's default download dir plus the multi-file subfolder rule (`name` for ≥2 files, none for single-file). This breaks for any per-torrent `output_folder` override and duplicates logic that already lives in `Session`.

### Request

A public accessor, e.g.:

```rust
impl ManagedTorrent {
    /// The resolved on-disk folder this torrent's files are written under.
    pub fn output_folder(&self) -> &Path {
        &self.shared().options.output_folder
    }
}
```

A per-file absolute-path helper (joining `output_folder` with each `FileInfo::relative_filename`) would be an even nicer convenience, but the folder accessor alone unblocks the use case (a UI that opens/reveals downloaded files).

When creating a Session like this:

```rust
let session = Session::new_with_opts(
    Default::default(),
    SessionOptions {
        listen: Some(ListenerOptions {
            listen_addr: "[::]:40000".parse().unwrap(),
            ..Default::default()
        }),
        ..Default::default()
    },
) 
```
netstat shows:
```
  TCP    0.0.0.0:40000          0.0.0.0:0              LISTENING       
  TCP    [::]:40000             [::]:0                 LISTENING       
```

However, the client only announces a single IPv6 address, even though it also has an IPv4 address.
Accordding to [BEP-7: IPv6 Tracker Extension](https://www.bittorrent.org/beps/bep_0007.html), it should announce for each local IP addresses.

